.TH "GOTO.SH" "1" "2026-07-25" "goto.sh 1.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
goto.sh, goto_trap.sh \- real forward and backward GOTO for bash
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBsource goto.sh\fR                    (first lines of a program)
\fBsource goto.sh --lib\fR              (define the API, take nothing over)
\fBgoto.sh\fR \fIprog.sh\fR [\fIargs\fR ...]
\fBgoto.sh \-E\fR \fIprog.sh\fR
\fBgoto.sh\fR < \fIprog.sh\fR
\fBgoto.sh \-h\fR | \fB\-V\fR
\fB#!/path/to/goto.sh\fR                (as a shebang interpreter)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B goto.sh
gives bash programs a real
.BR goto :
forward, backward, or computed, with full shell state preserved across
jumps (no re-exec, no lost variables, no lost file descriptors).
.PP
It is a source-to-source compiler. The program is parsed by bash itself
(via
.B eval
into a function body, read back canonically with
.BR "declare \-f" ),
scanned, validated, and rewritten into a single
.B case
dispatch trampoline in which every
.B goto
becomes a pc assignment plus a
.B continue
.I N
computed at compile time. Because the pc is a string dispatched by
.BR case ,
computed gotos cost nothing extra.
.SH PROGRAM SYNTAX
.TP
.BI "label " NAME
Jump target. Must sit at the top level of the program (not inside a loop,
function, or block), like a C label at function scope.
.TP
.BI "# label " NAME\fR,\fB " #: " NAME
The same, written as a comment.
.TP
.BI "goto " NAME
Jump to the label. Forward, backward, or computed:
.BR "goto \(dq$dest\(dq" .
Computed targets must be quoted; static targets are validated at compile
time, computed ones by the trampoline at run time. The command must not
have an assignment or leading-redirection prefix.
.TP
.BI "gosub " NAME " \fR...\fB ret"
Subroutine call with a real return stack.
.B gosub
must be top-level;
.B ret
may appear anywhere, including inside loops.
.PP
The four words are also defined as ordinary functions, so an uncompiled
run of the program still parses:
.B label
is a no-op and
.B goto
reports that the file was never compiled (status 127; execution
continues, since a stub cannot stop the caller).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-emit " " \fIprog.sh\fR
Compile and print the trampoline instead of running it.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the header documentation.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print name and version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GOTO_EMIT=1
Print the compiled program to stderr before running it.
.TP
.B GOTO_STRICT=0
Disable the runtime subshell guard (both runtimes). By default a
.B goto
executed inside
.BR "$( )" ,
.BR "( )" ,
or a pipeline is a fatal error, because the jump could never reach the
trampoline.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The compiled program's own exit status is propagated, including the
status of its final command. Otherwise: 2 for compile-time or usage
errors, 70 when the subshell guard fires (the guarded shell is sent
SIGTERM, so the status the parent reports is typically 143), and 127
returned \(em not exited \(em by the uncompiled
.B goto
stub.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Compile time, each fatal with exit 2:
.nf
goto.sh: error: goto to undefined label: NAME
goto.sh: error: gosub to undefined label: NAME
goto.sh: error: duplicate label NAME
goto.sh: error: label NAME is not at the top level of the program
goto.sh: error: label NAME uses the compiler's reserved __gt_/__GOTO_ namespace
goto.sh: error: goto target TEXT is not a valid label name
goto.sh: error: goto cannot cross a subshell or command substitution
goto.sh: error: `goto` inside a `...` command substitution can never jump
goto.sh: error: goto inside a function body cannot leave it
goto.sh: error: `goto` without a target
goto.sh: error: `goto` takes exactly one target
goto.sh: error: `ret` takes no arguments
goto.sh: error: `goto` cannot have an assignment or redirection prefix
goto.sh: error: label must be a plain `label NAME` at the top level
goto.sh: error: `break` outside any loop would hijack the trampoline (line: TEXT)
goto.sh: error: gosub NAME must be at the top level (it needs a return label)
goto.sh: error: gosub must be a plain `gosub NAME` at the top level
goto.sh: syntax error in program:
.fi
.PP
Run time:
.nf
goto: no such label: NAME            (computed goto miss; exit 2)
goto: fatal: `goto NAME` executed in a subshell or pipeline
goto: ret with empty gosub stack     (exit 2)
goto: missing label                  (trap runtime; exit 2)
goto: expected exactly one label     (trap runtime; exit 2)
goto_trap.sh: duplicate label: NAME  (exit 2)
.fi
.SH THE TRAP RUNTIME
.B goto_trap.sh
is an independent implementation with no compiler pass:
.B source goto_trap.sh
indexes label line numbers, then a DEBUG-trap longjmp (via
.BR "shopt \-s extdebug" )
unwinds one frame per command until the tail-eval trampoline re-evaluates
the program from the target line. It can do the one thing the compiled
runtime cannot \(em jump out of a called function, including recursive
ones \(em at the cost of a function call before every command, and it
owns the DEBUG trap and
.B extdebug
for the whole program. Same label/goto syntax and the same subshell
guard, but it does not implement
.B gosub
or
.BR ret ,
and its labels must also be top level: the trampoline re-evaluates the
program text from the label to end of file, so a label inside a loop or
block is a syntax error at jump time.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Labels must be top-level;
.B goto
is function-local and a
.B goto
inside a function body is rejected at compile time (use the trap runtime
if you need to jump out of a call); a
.B goto
cannot cross a subshell or pipeline; bare
.BR break / continue
outside your own loops is rejected;
.B $LINENO
and
.B BASH_SOURCE
inside the compiled program refer to the eval; reading the program from
stdin consumes stdin. The shebang-interpreter form requires a kernel
that accepts scripts as interpreters (Linux yes, macOS no). Names
starting with
.BR __gt_ ,
.BR __GOTO_ ,
and
.B __GT_
are reserved. Requires bash 5 or newer.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#!/usr/bin/env bash
source ./goto.sh

i=0
label top
echo "iteration $i"
(( i++ ))
(( i < 3 )) && goto top

goto "state_$USER_CHOICE"      # computed goto (quoted)
.fi
.PP
Heredoc form, running in the current shell:
.nf
source goto.sh --lib
eval "$(goto_compile <<'EOF'
\&...program...
EOF
)"
.fi
